# The protected Graph contract. `ITtscGraphApplication.ts` and every public
# subordinate type here, JSDoc included, are immutable by owner instruction, and
# the file is deliberately not Prettier-clean. The repository-wide sweep rewrote
# it anyway — reflowing prose the owner fixed — which is the failure mode #809
# names ("running the repository format script silently breaks the constraint").
# Re-pinning after each sweep would turn an owner instruction into a record of
# whatever the formatter last did, so the directory is exempt instead. A
# deliberate contract change still edits these files; the formatter does not.
packages/graph/src/structures/

# Generated by packages/ttsc/scripts/gen-flags.mts. Prettier's markdown parser
# mangles the leading `{/* ... */}` MDX comment (escapes the asterisks and joins
# the lines), which breaks Nextra's acorn parse. Leave it to the generator.
website/src/content/docs/ttsc/flags.mdx

# Everything the benchmark delivers to a measured agent byte-for-byte. The
# template is another repository's source and carries `{{token}}` placeholders
# the runner substitutes: Prettier reads `{{name}}` in a `.tsx` as an object
# literal and writes `{{ name }}`, which no longer matches the token and arrives
# as JSX that does not type-check. The requirements and instructions are the
# frozen inputs a run is identified by, so reflowing their tables changes what
# the agent read and what the cell is comparable against. Same reason as
# packages/graph/src/structures above: a formatter must not decide the contents
# of a frozen artifact. The benchmark's own `src` is this repository's code and
# stays formatted.
benchmarks/evidence/template/
benchmarks/evidence/requirements/
benchmarks/evidence/instructions/

# Measured workspaces, which are the run itself. `pnpm format` writes, so a
# formatter run during a campaign rewrites source inside workspaces that agents
# are editing at that moment — changing the artifact under measurement and the
# diff a reviewer judges it by. Being gitignored is no protection: the write
# still lands, it simply leaves no trace in `git status`. Nothing here is this
# repository's code, and a sweep over it also walks tens of thousands of files
# for nothing.
benchmarks/evidence/output/
